A poor young wife sells her beautiful hair to buy her husband a Christmas gift, only to learn he sold his own treasure to buy hers, and their useless gifts become the wisest love of all.

1. Poverty and Two Treasures  (passages 1-2)
    Poor Della has almost no money for Christmas but desperately wants a fine gift for Jim,
    whose watch and her long hair are their only prides.
       -> Having almost no money but wanting something worthy forces Della to rush out to find
       another way to pay for a gift.
2. Selling Hair for Chain  (passages 3-3)
    Della sells her long hair for twenty dollars to buy a fine platinum chain for Jim's watch,
    then fixes her short hair at home.
       -> With her hair cut short and the new chain hidden at home, Della anxiously waits to see
       if Jim will still find her pretty.
3. Shocked Stare and Reassurance  (passages 4-5)
    Jim stares in shock at Della's short hair, reassures her his love is unchanged, and gives
    her a package that swings her from joy to tears.
       -> Her joy turning to tears when she opens Jim's package demands to learn what he gave
       and what it cost him.
4. Useless Gifts Wisest Love  (passages 6-6)
    Jim sold his watch to buy Della combs she can no longer use, just as her chain has no watch,
    and their foolish sacrifices are called the wisest giving.
